WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.890
Even though Hollywood had a lot of success
in the 2010s, the decade also had plenty of

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:06.410
cinematic trainwrecks.

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:11.060
From superhero slip-ups to animated atrocities,
these are the biggest box office bombs of

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:12.060
the last decade.

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:16.640
Directed by Carl Rinsch, 47 Ronin arrived
with an impressive pedigree.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:20.920
This Keanu Reeves action movie wasn't just
an adaptation of a real-world historical event,

00:00:20.929 --> 00:00:24.550
in which 47 masterless samurai avenged the
death of their master.

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:29.220
Instead, it was merely one of many adaptations
of that very same event, often referred to

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:30.429
as the "Ako Incident."

00:00:30.429 --> 00:00:35.550
So 47 Ronin didn't just have a cool plot,
it had centuries of proven success as everything

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:38.600
from lavish stage productions to simple puppet
shows.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:42.440
Basically, 47 Ronin has a time-tested story
that you can bank on.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:44.060
And yet it tanked.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:48.580
The movie missed every target that mattered,
opening ninth in the US and delivering dismal

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:49.930
numbers in Japan.

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:53.720
There are many reasons as to why, with the
most obvious being the decision to insert

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:55.520
fantasy elements into the tale.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:58.030
In retrospect, this is the most baffling choice.

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:02.440
Did a story already beloved for being about
awesomely talented warriors out for blood

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:05.640
really need a CGI fox lady and birdlike monks?

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.600
Hollywood thought so and suffered for it.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.840
47 Ronin ended up losing Universal an estimated
$175 million.

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:16.420
Some critics blamed the script, while others
thought the acting was a bit wooden.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:18.440
"I have come for your help.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.340
We are in need of swords.”

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:23.500
“Then take it."

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:30.200
In the end, it just goes to show that there
are no sure things when it comes to movies,

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:34.820
especially when you try to cram dragons into
a classic story of 18th-century heroism.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.360
Roald Dahl has generally provided Hollywood
with winning source material.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.120
Charlie and the Chocolate Factory has been
the basis for two popular films, James and

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:47.520
the Giant Peach is a stop-motion delight,
and Matilda is a touchstone for '90s kids

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:48.220
everywhere.

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:53.120
Yet somehow, the film version of The BFG,
as beloved as any of Dahl's other stories,

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.580
flopped like a giant being dumped in the ocean.

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:59.820
Having Steven Spielberg at the helm didn't
save it and generally positive reviews didn't help.

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:04.420
It was a box office bomb in spite of it all,
leaving a trail of questions in its wake.

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:08.009
Why weren't audiences raised on similar Dahl
adaptations eating this one up?

00:02:08.009 --> 00:02:13.520
How did a spectacle of this nature manage
to bring in only $18.7 million over its opening weekend?

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:19.980
How did its final gross of $183.3 million
only barely clear its budget of $140 million?

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:23.860
The only question this film really answers
is "What would happen if Queen Elizabeth and

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:25.400
her dogs drank like a giant?"

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:38.880
One can't help but wonder if Spielberg has
simply lost his touch when it comes to children's movies.

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:42.880
Though his The Adventures of Tintin made an
impressive amount of money and received largely

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:47.640
positive reviews, it hasn't entered the canon
of the best Spielberg movies in any substantive way.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:49.360
It was fun, but not monumental.

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:53.290
Seen in contrast, The BFG feels like a further
slide down that hill.

00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:58.269
The BFG is a fine film, but it's not an event,
and audiences chose to spend their money elsewhere.

00:02:58.269 --> 00:03:00.740
Oh well, at least there's always Matilda.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:04.880
Ah, to be a boy discovering the joys of his
first car.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:08.420
It's a classic American story about creative
drive and personal freedoms.

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:12.600
Similarly beloved are stories about boys and
their dogs.They become best friends across

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:15.860
the lines of species, forging an understanding
all their own.

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:17.640
You know what else people enjoy?

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:20.160
Weird monsters, especially when it comes to
movies.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.640
So was Monster Trucks really such a bad idea?

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:27.280
It combined all three of these tropes into
a movie about a boy and a creature who uses

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:30.230
the shell of a pickup truck as a makeshift
exoskeleton.

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:33.200
Theoretically no, but practically... yes.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:37.360
Monster Trucks had a long, strange trip to
the theater, beginning as the first film of

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:42.359
a franchise that ended up evaporating, then
having its release date shifted multiple times.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:46.640
By the time it premiered, Paramount was already
expecting to lose money, and it did.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:51.480
Reviews were unkind, and the worldwide gross
only cracked $64.5 million.

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:55.520
Monster Trucks, for all its seemingly safe
bets, finished behind the horror trainwreck

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:57.800
The Bye Bye Man in its opening weekend.

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:01.920
Ticket buyers might love stories about aliens,
cars, and dogs but as Hollywood should know

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:04.920
by now, they might not love them all in one
movie.

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:09.940
Take a moment to really let the concept of
a movie entitled Cowboys &amp; Aliens sink in.

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:14.180
It's a film title that absolutely rules, and
it comes with a good degree of flexibility.

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:18.180
The on-the-nose nature of it allows for a
sense of fun that doesn't threaten the spectacle

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:20.400
of, well, cowboys and aliens.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:24.300
Add in Daniel Craig and Harrison Ford and
you've got yourself a blockbuster.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:25.780
Yet none of that mattered.

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:29.960
Cowboys &amp; Aliens was a genuine bomb from its
lackluster opening day.

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:30.820
What went wrong?

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:35.139
Reviews weren't terrible, but their middling
nature reveals the film's fatal flaw: A movie

00:04:35.139 --> 00:04:38.240
called Cowboys &amp; Aliens really can't afford
to play it safe.

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:42.280
Though some reviewers felt the film's insistence
upon playing its absurd premise straight was

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:46.020
a strength, more found it to be a choice that
hamstrung the production from the get-go.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:50.470
Ultimately, there are better cowboy movies,
better alien invasion movies, better Daniel

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:53.060
Craig movies, and better Harrison Ford movies.

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:57.780
The fact that the film barely cleared its
$163 million budget with a worldwide total

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:03.470
of $174.8 million, isn't much of a surprise,
nor the fact that the movie fizzled out long

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:06.480
before anyone seriously considered the possibility
of a sequel.

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:08.700
"I don't want to do that again.”

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:09.500
“No."

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:15.960
Superheroes are omnipresent, and Marvel is
the goose that laid the golden eggs...for

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:16.820
the most part.

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:21.620
Consider the Fantastic Four, a longtime cornerstone
of the Marvel Comics universe.

00:05:21.639 --> 00:05:26.150
Their movies, historically, haven't amounted
to much, with the 2005 film being the high-water

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:29.340
mark as a good-enough jaunt that brought in
good-enough money.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:33.349
But the team was owned by 20th Century Fox
until recently, and it was consigned to a

00:05:33.349 --> 00:05:36.780
2015 reboot meant to compete with the dominant
MCU.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:41.540
Sadly, for the Fantastic Four, this competition
ended in failure and reviewers had plenty

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:43.090
of things to say about the film.

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:45.960
However, there is one thing that absolutely
no one said…

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:48.100
"It's fantastic."

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:50.160
Why all the criticism?

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:54.180
It was a terrible movie, as dour as it was
overcomplicated.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:58.199
Fantastic Four felt like the sort of adaptation
a 14-year-old might make after being mocked

00:05:58.200 --> 00:05:59.620
for liking comic books.

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:04.740
Add in lopsided pacing and a convoluted plot,
and you end up with the lowest-grossing Fantastic

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:09.800
Four film of all time, a movie that lost the
studio an estimated $100 million bucks.

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:13.520
But now that some time has passed, will the
MCU revive the Fantastic Four?

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:14.460
We can only hope.

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:20.340
As far as the 2015 reboot goes, fans, critics,
and probably some of cast want to forget that

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:21.350
it ever happened.

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:23.320
"That, we can agree on…”

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:29.580
Orson Scott Card's 1985 novel, Ender's Game,
has been a classic for decades.

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:33.129
How many thousands of people found their footing
in the world of science fiction through Ender

00:06:33.129 --> 00:06:34.199
Wiggins' story?

00:06:34.199 --> 00:06:38.500
How many recall the shocking ending as one
of the greatest literary twists of their lives?

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:41.180
The numbers are untold, but the impact is
clear.

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:44.080
A movie based on Ender's Game was primed for
success.

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:47.640
Somehow, however, 2013's Ender's Game was
a dud.

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:51.450
Despite the wealth of marketing tie-ins, a
script for a sequel ready to go, and some

00:06:51.450 --> 00:06:57.509
pretty solid reviews, the movie grossed a
mere $125 million, only barely clearing its

00:06:57.509 --> 00:06:59.210
$110 million budget.

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:02.780
The reasons for these numbers are less obvious
than other, similar bombs.

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:07.460
Ender's Game was solidly entertaining, visually
engaging, based on a celebrated book, and

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:10.780
released for a public that couldn't get enough
of teen dystopias.

00:07:10.789 --> 00:07:14.860
It seems that the movie was simply less than
the sum of its parts, a bit too late to the

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:19.610
young adult franchise fad, maybe a bit too
old in its source material to intrigue today's

00:07:19.610 --> 00:07:22.781
audiences, and likely biting off a bit more
than it could chew, story-wise.

00:07:22.781 --> 00:07:27.510
What's certain is that it failed and studio
execs probably wish they could go back in

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:30.320
time and ask themselves one very important
question.

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:32.800
"What are you going to do?

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:35.940
Waste millions on a loser?"

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:40.539
Clearly, there won't be any sequels and it
looks like fans will have to content themselves

00:07:40.540 --> 00:07:44.080
with Ender's adventures on the written page…
which is still pretty awesome.

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:48.919
For a species honed by years of evolution
to survive against all odds, humans sure do

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:51.200
love seeing themselves in mortal peril.

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:52.560
Consider disaster movies.

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:56.620
They're explicitly centered around watching
people die at the hands of forces too huge

00:07:56.620 --> 00:08:00.720
and unknowable to ever control, and the genre
is one of our most beloved.

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:04.759
Some come as a result of man's overreach,
like Titanic, while others, like Twister,

00:08:04.759 --> 00:08:07.129
are merely the wrath of the winds themselves.

00:08:07.129 --> 00:08:11.909
Geostorm combined these approaches by imagining
a global cataclysm created by malfunctioning

00:08:11.909 --> 00:08:16.129
satellites that humanity, in their arrogance,
installed to control the weather.

00:08:16.129 --> 00:08:20.939
Tokyo is battered by a monster hail storm,
pockets of Afghanistan freeze over, and on

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:24.500
and on it goes, taking the audience on a tour
of worldwide destruction.

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:27.120
Critics ripped it apart, and with good reason.

00:08:27.129 --> 00:08:32.099
Geostorm is remarkably boring for a movie
of such epic proportions, lacking the jaw-dropping

00:08:32.099 --> 00:08:34.780
visuals that are the entire point of the genre.

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:37.390
And the film's humans are nothing to write
home about either.

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:41.180
Though many critically derided movies have
gone on to make money, Geostorm didn't even

00:08:41.180 --> 00:08:43.540
come close to Warner Brothers' expectations.

00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:46.140
"It's worse than we thought.”

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:47.340
“Yeah, I think we've established that."

00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:51.900
Pixar is as close to a watchword for quality
as the movie industry gets.

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:56.000
Almost all of their films are commercial and
critical successes...and many are considered

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:57.220
modern classics.

00:08:57.220 --> 00:09:01.560
They were featuring superheroes before Marvel
brought spandex back into vogue, making monsters

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:07.339
lovable years ahead of Monster High, and introducing
kids to sophisticated French cuisine before...well,

00:09:07.339 --> 00:09:09.339
they're still ahead of the pack on that one.

00:09:09.339 --> 00:09:12.339
But no enterprise is immune to the occasional
mistake.

00:09:12.339 --> 00:09:16.800
Enter The Good Dinosaur, Pixar's tale of an
Apatosaurus and his boy in an alternate Earth

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:18.800
where dinosaurs never went extinct.

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:22.980
It's got all the conceptual charm of a Pixar
knockout, from an unlikely friendship to an

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:25.020
early heartbreak that must be surpassed.

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:26.420
But something was missing.

00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:30.640
The reviews weren't bad, but they weren't
quite up to Pixar's usual standard, and the

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:32.300
box office followed suit.

00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:38.540
The film only made $332.2 million, remarkably
short of the film's $350 million marketing

00:09:38.540 --> 00:09:41.860
and production budget...which by any standard
is an epic failure.

00:09:42.150 --> 00:09:43.480
“Dang!”

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:44.220
“Oh!!'

00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:49.000
Perhaps part of its lackluster ticket sales
can be chalked up to Inside Out being released

00:09:49.019 --> 00:09:53.560
a few months prior or the extensive revisions
the film underwent on its way to release.

00:09:53.560 --> 00:09:58.260
Or maybe, just maybe, nobody wanted to see
a film that co-starred a grunting cave-toddler.

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:00.660
"This is all your fault!"

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:03.780
Justice League debuted at an odd moment for DC.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:08.040
The studio's struggles to produce a decent
film had not yet been eclipsed by the successes

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:09.040
of Shazam!

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:10.430
And Joaquin Phoenix's Joker.

00:10:10.430 --> 00:10:12.800
Only Wonder Woman had managed to impress critics.

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:16.520
With so much competition from Marvel, Justice
League had to be a mission statement and a

00:10:16.529 --> 00:10:20.480
course correction, all the while introducing
the Flash, Cyborg, and Aquaman.

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:24.459
The fact that it disappointed isn't surprising
in light of this, but it doesn't mean it stung

00:10:24.460 --> 00:10:25.360
any less.

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:30.700
In retrospect, Justice League never had much
hope of recouping even its $300 million production budget.

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:35.820
It's one of the most expensive movies ever
made, and it needed to bring in at least $750

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:40.120
million to break even with all associated
costs, but not even that benchmark could be

00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:40.820
cleared.

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:44.400
Its worldwide gross landed at $657 million.

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:48.579
Reviews for the film were pretty lukewarm,
which was better than what Dawn of Justice

00:10:48.579 --> 00:10:52.070
got, but still not enough to send the world
to the cinema in droves.

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:56.300
Weighted down with expectations, Justice League
chugged forward as best it could.

00:10:56.300 --> 00:11:00.350
But in Hollywood, one's best isn't always
good enough, even when it features Batman

00:11:00.350 --> 00:11:01.730
striking cool poses.

00:11:01.730 --> 00:11:04.370
Still, Justice League didn't disappoint everyone.

00:11:04.370 --> 00:11:07.080
We're sure it was loved by plenty of hardcore
fans.

00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:08.840
"Not enough…”

